VERSION CHOICE / IMPORTANT
--------------------------
This guide uses Java 11+, Apache Tomcat 9, Maven, MySQL/MariaDB and the
javax.servlet API (Servlet 4.0), JSP 2.3 and JSTL 1.2.
Tomcat 9 uses javax.servlet.*. Tomcat 10/10.1 uses jakarta.servlet.*.
Do not mix Tomcat 10 with the javax imports in these examples.

The practical question list does not specify versions. These are explicit
compatibility assumptions so the examples can be used together.
These are classroom demonstrations, not production-ready security code.


NETBEANS 15 + TOMCAT 9 SETUP
----------------------------
This practical guide is compatible with Apache NetBeans 15 and Apache Tomcat 9.
Use a JDK supported by NetBeans 15 (JDK 11 or JDK 17 is a suitable choice).

1. Install / check:
   - Apache NetBeans 15 with Java Web / Jakarta EE support
   - JDK 11 or JDK 17
   - Apache Tomcat 9
   - MariaDB or MySQL

2. Register Tomcat 9 in NetBeans:
   - Open NetBeans.
   - Go to Tools > Servers > Add Server.
   - Select Apache Tomcat or TomEE (Tomcat 9).
   - Browse to the Tomcat 9 installation folder.
   - Enter the Tomcat Manager username and password if requested.
   - Finish. Tomcat should appear under the Services > Servers node.

   If Tomcat Manager credentials are required, edit:
       TOMCAT_HOME/conf/tomcat-users.xml
   Add these lines inside the <tomcat-users> element:
       <role rolename="manager-script"/>
       <user username="netbeans" password="NetBeansLab123!" roles="manager-script"/>
   Restart Tomcat after changing this file. Use these credentials only for a
   local college lab; do not expose the Manager application to the internet.

3. Create the project:
   - File > New Project.
   - Choose Java with Maven > Web Application.
   - Project Name: java-practicals
   - Group ID: com.example
   - Version: 1.0
   - Select Apache Tomcat 9 as the server if the wizard asks.
   - Finish.

   If the wizard does not ask for a server, right-click the project >
   Properties > Run and select the registered Tomcat 9 server if available.

4. Add the code:
   - Java classes: Source Packages > com.example.
   - HTML and JSP files: Web Pages.
   - Replace the generated pom.xml dependencies with the pom.xml in this guide,
     or merge the listed dependencies into the generated pom.xml.
   - Create DB.java in package com.example.
   - Keep the package name com.example for all servlet classes.

5. Run:
   - Right-click the project > Clean and Build.
   - Right-click the project > Run.
   - NetBeans builds the WAR and deploys it to Tomcat.
   - Open the URL shown in the Output window, normally:
       http://localhost:8080/java-practicals/

   After changing files, use Run again. To view errors, open the Output window
   and check Tomcat logs under TOMCAT_HOME/logs/catalina.out.

IMPORTANT:
- This guide uses javax.servlet.* and JSTL 1.2, which match Tomcat 9.
- Do not change imports to jakarta.servlet.* when using Tomcat 9.
- If NetBeans generated a different Java source/target version in pom.xml,
  set maven.compiler.source and maven.compiler.target to 11 (or a JDK level
  supported by the installed compiler).
- The guide's Maven commands still work, but NetBeans Run is the easier
  deployment workflow when the Tomcat server is registered.

